[{"id":"q-4","category":"technical","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Explain the difference between ArrayList and LinkedList.","expectedAnswerPoints":["Underlying Structure: ArrayList uses a dynamically resizing array; LinkedList is built upon a doubly linked list of nodes.","Random Access: ArrayList provides O(1) constant-time random access by index via direct memory offset; LinkedList requires O(N) traversal.","Insertion/Deletion: LinkedList offers O(1) insertion/deletion at endpoints or when an iterator is positioned; ArrayList requires O(N) array copying/shifting.","Memory Overhead: LinkedList consumes more memory per element due to node pointers (prev and next references)."],"evaluationCriteria":["Big-O time complexity comparison for search vs insertion/deletion","Memory layout differences (contiguous memory array vs pointer overhead)","Practical guidance on when to choose one over the other in production"],"answer":""},{"id":"q-8","category":"technical","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the difference between authentication and authorization?","expectedAnswerPoints":["Authentication (AuthN) verifies WHO a user is — validating their identity through credentials (passwords, MFA tokens, biometrics, OAuth tokens).","Authorization (AuthZ) determines WHAT permissions and resources an authenticated user is permitted to access (RBAC, ABAC, role scopes like ROLE_ADMIN vs ROLE_STUDENT).","Workflow sequence: Authentication must always precede authorization.","Implementation mechanisms: Authentication uses passwords/session cookies/JWT tokens; Authorization checks role claims or ACL policies."],"evaluationCriteria":["Crisp definition: Identity verification vs privilege enforcement","Proper sequencing (authentication before authorization)","Practical architectural tools (JWT claims, Spring Security @PreAuthorize, RBAC)"],"answer":""},{"id":"q-15","category":"coding","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Find the second-largest element in an array.","expectedAnswerPoints":["Single-pass O(N) approach maintaining two tracking variables: largest and secondLargest.","Update largest when element > largest; shift former largest to secondLargest.","Update secondLargest when element > secondLargest and element != largest.","Handle duplicates properly so that duplicate largest values do not mask the real distinct second largest."],"evaluationCriteria":["Single-pass O(N) time with O(1) space instead of sorting in O(N log N)","Proper handling of arrays with fewer than 2 elements (throwing exception or returning Optional)","Handling of all-identical elements (e.g. [5, 5, 5, 5])"],"codeTemplate":"public class SecondLargestFinder {
    public static Integer findSecondLargest(int[] arr) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {
            return null; // Not enough elements
        }

        int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        int secondLargest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        boolean foundDistinct = false;

        for (int num : arr) {
            if (num > largest) {
                if (largest != Integer.MIN_VALUE) {
                    secondLargest = largest;
                    foundDistinct = true;
                }
                largest = num;
            } else if (num > secondLargest && num != largest) {
                secondLargest = num;
                foundDistinct = true;
            }
        }

        return foundDistinct ? secondLargest : null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {12, 35, 1, 10, 34, 1};
        System.out.println("Second Largest: " + findSecondLargest(nums));
    }
}","defaultLanguage":"java","answer":""},{"id":"q-17","category":"coding","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Find duplicate elements in an integer array.","expectedAnswerPoints":["Approach 1: HashSet lookup in O(N) time and O(N) space. Iterate through array; if set.add(num) returns false, it is a duplicate.","Approach 2: Sort the array in O(N log N) time and O(1) auxiliary space, checking adjacent elements.","Approach 3: If array values are bounded from 1 to N, use array index negation for O(N) time and O(1) space."],"evaluationCriteria":["Time and space complexity trade-offs explained clearly","Handling of multiple duplicates without returning duplicate entries in the output list","Clean set-based implementation"],"codeTemplate":"import java.util.*;

public class DuplicateFinder {
    public static Set<Integer> findDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        Set<Integer> duplicates = new LinkedHashSet<>();

        for (int num : nums) {
            if (!seen.add(num)) {
                duplicates.add(num);
            }
        }
        return duplicates;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {4, 3, 2, 7, 8, 2, 3, 1};
        System.out.println("Duplicates: " + findDuplicates(arr));
    }
}","defaultLanguage":"java","answer":""},{"id":"q-19","category":"resume","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Explain one project you developed and the problem it solves.","expectedAnswerPoints":["Core problem statement and business/user context.","Target user demographic and why existing solutions were inadequate.","High-level architectural overview: frontend interface, backend service, and data store.","Concrete outcomes or measurable impact achieved by the project."],"evaluationCriteria":["Clear, articulate problem statement using STAR framework","Architectural clarity connecting technical components","Evidence of real engineering ownership"],"answer":""},{"id":"q-20","category":"resume","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Describe your role, technical contributions, and challenges in your project.","expectedAnswerPoints":["Specific responsibilities (e.g., API design, database schema, state management, deployment pipeline).","Key technical decisions made individually vs in collaboration with team members.","A notable technical roadblock encountered and the systematic debugging approach taken to overcome it."],"evaluationCriteria":["Precise identification of personal contribution vs team effort","Technical depth in explaining engineering hurdles","Structured problem-solving mindset"],"answer":""},{"id":"q-26","category":"behavioral","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Describe a technical challenge you faced and how you solved it.","expectedAnswerPoints":["Situation: The technical environment and constraint (e.g. deadline, unfamiliar library, failing build).","Task: What needed to be delivered.","Action: Proactive steps taken to research, prototype, debug, and implement the solution.","Result: Concrete outcome (feature delivered, latency reduced, test suite passed)."],"evaluationCriteria":["Structured response adhering to the STAR method","Technical problem solving and resourcefulness demonstrated","Quantifiable or clear qualitative result"],"answer":"